from pathlib import Path
P=Path('/home/duyennh/AI_projects/research_lidar/Lidar/MobilePIXORNeXt__Compact_LiDAR_Object_Detection_via_Strata_BEV_Encoding_and_Oriented_Geometric_Alignment')
f=P/'sections/results.tex';s=f.read_text();lines=s.splitlines()
def para(prefix,new):
    hits=[i for i,l in enumerate(lines) if l.startswith(prefix)];assert len(hits)==1,(prefix,hits);lines[hits[0]]=new
para('Table~\\ref{tab:comparison} presents',r'Table~\ref{tab:comparison} separates local loader-aligned BEV results from reference-evaluator results. The shared nominal IoU thresholds and $R_{40}$ interpolation do not remove differences in ground-truth filtering, matching, and ignored-object handling. The comparison therefore provides implementation context rather than a common accuracy leaderboard.')
para('\\caption{Pareto trade-off',r'\caption{Archived accuracy, parameter-count, and offline-latency context. Panel (a) shows Moderate mAP versus parameter count; panel (b) shows mAP versus pipeline latency. Local and reference AP values use different evaluators, and the MobilePIXOR timing is from a different GPU. Consequently, the plotted frontier is illustrative and does not establish cross-protocol Pareto dominance. The local L4 accuracy snapshot and T4 profiling snapshot for MobilePIXORNeXt are distinguished in the text.}')
para('As visualized in the Pareto',r'MobilePIXORNeXt contains $753{,}569$ deployable parameters ($0.754\,\text{M}$) and 12 training-only logits. The seed-42 L4 report gives $71.50\%$ Moderate mAP and $73.45\%$ $\text{mAP}_9$, while the separate T4 report gives $71.46\%$ and $73.42\%$. Relative to the parameter counts of PointPillars~\cite{lang2019pointpillars} and SECOND~\cite{yan2018second}, the reductions are $84.41\%$ and $85.85\%$. These are parameter-count comparisons; the heterogeneous AP values in Figure~\ref{fig:pareto_tradeoff} do not establish an accuracy advantage.')
para('\\caption{Precision--recall curves',r'\caption{Archived precision--recall curves on the KITTI validation split. Columns show Car ($\tau_{\mathrm{IoU}}=0.70$), Pedestrian ($0.50$), and Cyclist ($0.50$); rows show Easy, Moderate, and Hard. MobilePIXOR and MobilePIXORNeXt use the local evaluator, whereas PIXOR and the external 3D detectors use the reference evaluator. Colors identify the models as indicated by the legend. Curves from these protocol groups are descriptive and cannot establish a shared accuracy ranking.}')
para('Detecting vulnerable road users',r'The local L4 report gives $60.78\%$ Pedestrian Moderate AP for MobilePIXORNeXt and $28.16\%$ for the historical MobilePIXOR checkpoint. The numerical difference is $32.62\,\text{pp}$, but architecture, input representation, and training history all differ. SECOND and PointPillars report $56.56\%$ and $56.01\%$ under the reference evaluator; these values do not establish that the local detector is the best single-stage pedestrian detector.')
para('In addition to vulnerable road users',r'MobilePIXORNeXt records $86.90\%$ Car and $66.81\%$ Cyclist Moderate AP in the same local L4 evaluation. Its $73.45\%$ nine-cell mean summarizes the three classes across Easy, Moderate, and Hard. Per-class and per-difficulty results, rather than the aggregate alone, are needed to assess how errors vary across object types and visibility conditions.')
para('To verify that the empirical gains',r'Table~\ref{tab:seed_robustness} summarizes the three archived runs for each detector. MobilePIXORNeXt has a mean Moderate mAP of $70.94\pm0.89\%$ and $\text{mAP}_9$ of $73.01\pm0.68\%$, compared with $47.21\pm5.06\%$ and $48.55\pm5.45\%$ for MobilePIXOR. The descriptive mean differences are $23.73\,\text{pp}$ and $24.46\,\text{pp}$. These are sample summaries of the available records, rather than evidence from a fixed-budget seed experiment.')
para('Crucially, MobilePIXOR exhibits',r'The records differ beyond random initialization: MobilePIXOR seed 42 uses Binary35 and an incompletely archived training run, whereas seeds 43/44 use Hist14 and complete 100-epoch runs. The final detector also uses 160 epochs for seed 42 and 100 for seeds 43/44. Therefore, the observed standard deviations cannot isolate initialization sensitivity, and they do not identify LayerScale, T-SBUW, or PC-GTA as the cause of the dispersion difference.')
para('Network forward time covers only',r'Network time covers only part of the offline pipeline. Input preparation, H2D transfer, decoding, and NMS contribute additional latency. Two archived reports are available, with different GPUs and slightly different AP results:')
para('    \\item \\textbf{Standalone GPU',r'    \item \textbf{L4 model stage}: $8.67\,\text{ms}$ ($115.32\,\text{FPS}$) in the seed-42 L4 evaluation report. This value is not a standalone T4 measurement.')
para('    \\item \\textbf{Pipeline Synchronous',r'    \item \textbf{T4 model stage}: $17.88\,\text{ms}$ ($55.94\,\text{FPS}$) within the separate T4 profiling snapshot, whose offline pipeline totals $60.96\,\text{ms}$.')
para('Table~\\ref{tab:efficiency} reports sequential',r'Table~\ref{tab:efficiency} retains GPU identity and the measured network-stage boundary. The T4 rows use batch size 1 and 3,759 timed frames after 10 warmup frames. The historical MobilePIXOR row is from L4 and must not be used for a same-hardware speedup estimate.')
para('Table~\\ref{tab:efficiency} reports MobilePIXORNeXt',r'The T4 profiling snapshot records $60.96\,\text{ms}$ ($16.41\,\text{FPS}$) for MobilePIXORNeXt and $63.84\,\text{ms}$ ($15.66\,\text{FPS}$) for PointPillars. Their median latencies are $58.28\,\text{ms}$ and $62.10\,\text{ms}$. MobilePIXORNeXt has higher P95/P99 values ($80.85/95.91\,\text{ms}$) than PointPillars ($75.88/92.73\,\text{ms}$). Both archived P99 values are below $100\,\text{ms}$, but these offline statistics neither establish a worst-case deadline nor validate latency in closed-loop driving. No scene-density stratification is available to assign a cause to the tail.')
para('Stage-level profiling identified',r'The four profiled T4 stage means sum to $60.67\,\text{ms}$; the difference from the directly measured $60.96\,\text{ms}$ pipeline mean is $0.29\,\text{ms}$. This is a measurement residual, not a separately profiled metadata-processing stage:')
para('    \\item \\textbf{Host-to-Device Memory',r'    \item \textbf{Host-to-Device Transfer ($6.91\,\text{ms}$)}: The $800\times704\times14$ FP32 tensor requires $31.54\,\text{MB}$, compared with $78.85\,\text{MB}$ for 35 channels, a $60.0\%$ payload reduction. The historical Binary35 report records $40.63\,\text{ms}$ transfer time on L4. Hardware, memory pinning, and implementation differ, so the timing ratio cannot isolate the benefit of channel reduction.')
para('    \\item \\textbf{Model Forward Propagation',r'    \item \textbf{Model Stage ($17.88\,\text{ms}$)}: The T4 report records $55.94\,\text{FPS}$ for this stage. The separate $8.67\,\text{ms}$ value is from L4; no same-GPU isolated-versus-pipeline experiment establishes the cause of their difference.')
para('    \\item \\textbf{Decoding and Rotated',r'    \item \textbf{Decoding and Rotated NMS ($14.94\,\text{ms}$)}: This is the combined post-processing stage. It does not separately measure peak extraction, coordinate decoding, NMS, and output transfer.')
para('Because LiDAR beam divergence',r'LiDAR returns generally become sparser with increasing range. Table~\ref{tab:distance_ap} reports local Moderate AP for three distance intervals: $0\text{--}30\,\text{m}$, $30\text{--}50\,\text{m}$, and $50\text{--}70.4\,\text{m}$. These aggregate range results compare the full detector configurations and do not isolate a representation or augmentation mechanism.')
para('Compared with MobilePIXOR in the near-range',r'Within $0\text{--}30\,\text{m}$, MobilePIXORNeXt records $65.23\%$ Pedestrian AP versus $30.45\%$ for MobilePIXOR, a $34.78\,\text{pp}$ difference. Cyclist AP is $76.32\%$ versus $25.55\%$, a $50.77\,\text{pp}$ difference. These are gains for the integrated configurations under the local evaluator.')
para('At medium ranges',r'At $30\text{--}50\,\text{m}$, Pedestrian AP is $11.49\%$ versus $3.75\%$, and Cyclist AP is $44.30\%$ versus $12.61\%$. At $50\text{--}70.4\,\text{m}$, Cyclist AP is $14.94\%$ versus $0.56\%$. Far-range Pedestrian AP remains very low and decreases from $0.99\%$ to $0.80\%$ ($-0.19\,\text{pp}$). The results therefore show an uneven range benefit; neither the table nor the selected visual examples establishes the contribution of range-adaptive sampling or kernel width.')
para('To quantify the individual and cumulative',r'Table~\ref{tab:components} now records source run identities alongside the historical configurations. The records change multiple factors, training budgets, and selection criteria. They document the experimental trajectory rather than a controlled additive decomposition of the final detector.')
para('Replacing MobilePIXOR\'s residual',r'The archived B0 and B1 results are $41.40\%$ and $47.60\%$ Moderate mAP. B0 was archived at 38 of 100 planned epochs and uses an epoch-30 checkpoint; B1 completed 100 epochs and uses a loss-selected epoch-13 checkpoint. The $6.20\,\text{pp}$ difference is descriptive and cannot be attributed solely to replacing residual blocks.')
para('To assess how input rasterization',r'Table~\ref{tab:encoding} reports the early representation experiments. Strata-BEV14 reaches $50.14\%$ Moderate mAP, compared with $47.60\%$ for Binary35, $47.37\%$ for Strata-BEV8, and $44.42\%$ for the local TriBand-BEV adaptation. The 14-channel payload is $31.54\,\text{MB}$ versus $78.85\,\text{MB}$ for Binary35. Because Binary35 uses a different training budget and checkpoint-selection criterion, the $2.54\,\text{pp}$ AP difference is not a controlled estimate of the representation effect. These comparisons also change groups of input features together rather than isolating centroid offsets or elevation moments.')
para('Separating Car and Ped/Cyc',r'The grouped-H16 configuration G16 records $51.30\%$ Moderate mAP versus $50.14\%$ for the earlier single-head E14 configuration. The $60.22\%$ result assigned to A5 is O32, which uses grouped H32 heads, F32 fusion, and a simplified GT sampler. It changes augmentation and neck width as well as head width relative to G16. Accordingly, the $8.92\,\text{pp}$ difference does not establish a head-capacity effect, and no rank or gradient analysis demonstrates latent subspace collapse.')
para('Physics-consistent augmentation (',r'The result $66.25\%$ Moderate mAP belongs to O32G16: a simplified-sampler V2 run with H16 heads and F32 SG-FPN. It is not an H32/F24 PC-GTA addition to O32. The historical O32 and H32 runs share the recorded architecture and score $60.22\%$ and $68.12\%$, but also change sampler probability, quotas, and placement settings. Their $7.91\,\text{pp}$ difference therefore compares complete sampler recipes and does not isolate ground fitting, obstacle rejection, or range subsampling.')
para('Increasing neck width from 24',r'H32 records $68.12\%$ Moderate mAP using SumFPN, while S32 records $68.46\%$ using SG-FPN. Their insertion quotas also differ (6/8/10 versus 15/10/10), so the $0.34\,\text{pp}$ difference is not an isolated neck effect. The previously assigned A8 score of $67.38\%$ belongs instead to P1, which adds focal context. Table~\ref{tab:components} identifies it separately as A8F.')
para('Oriented Geometric Alignment (OGA) and',r'Within the earlier focal-context architecture, P1 and P4 score $67.38\%$ and $70.58\%$ Moderate mAP. This $3.20\,\text{pp}$ difference combines OGA and the IQA prediction/supervision/ranking branch. It does not demonstrate a specific interaction with SG-FPN or establish the gain of the same package in the final shared-head model.')
para('Embedding parameter-free Block-SimAM',r'The final shared-head seed-42 detector with Block-SimAM records $71.50\%$ Moderate mAP and $73.45\%$ $\text{mAP}_9$ in the L4 report. Its difference from historical P4 ($70.58\%$) is $0.92\,\text{pp}$, but P4 retains focal context and earlier independent towers and uses a different training budget. This is not an isolated Block-SimAM gain.')
para('Crucially, because both A9',r'Block-SimAM itself adds no learned attention parameters. A matched final-architecture run with only Block-SimAM disabled is needed to quantify its accuracy and latency contribution; no such comparison has been established by the listed records.')
para('Block-SimAM gained',r'The historical placement results in Table~\ref{tab:attention} also change neck width or configuration history. Their AP and speed differences are therefore not controlled placement effects.')
para('Table~\\ref{tab:loss} presents a',r'Table~\ref{tab:loss} reports a historical $2\times2$ OGA--IQA study with focal context and independent prediction towers retained in P1--P4. It is distinct from the final no-focal shared-head detector.')
para('Table~\\ref{tab:loss} reports the',r'Without IQA, OGA raises Moderate mAP from $67.38\%$ (P1) to $68.97\%$ (P2), a $1.59\,\text{pp}$ difference. The per-class differences, calculated from unrounded records, are $1.12\,\text{pp}$ for Car, $0.94\,\text{pp}$ for Pedestrian, and $2.71\,\text{pp}$ for Cyclist. Adding IQA to baseline supervision gives $70.31\%$ (P3); combining IQA with OGA gives $70.58\%$ (P4). OGA therefore adds only $0.27\,\text{pp}$ when IQA is present, compared with $1.59\,\text{pp}$ when absent. The factorial interaction on mAP is approximately $-1.32\,\text{pp}$, so these records do not demonstrate a positive additive synergy.')
para('Table~\\ref{tab:loss} compares explicit',r'P11 Q-OGA records $69.20\%$ Moderate mAP with $815{,}743$ detector parameters, versus $852{,}929$ for historical P4 with IQA. Q-OGA differs from P2 OGA in classification targets, corner smoothing, and range weighting; their $0.23\,\text{pp}$ difference cannot isolate quality coupling alone. The final detector uses different shared towers, so this historical AP must not be paired with a claimed $734{,}943$-parameter final Q-OGA model.')
para('Stage-level SimAM at downsampling',r'The historical stage-attention results are $69.44\%$, $69.80\%$, and $69.36\%$ Moderate mAP. The middle row uses F24 in the source record, unlike F32 in the final model; the no-attention P4 row also retains focal context. The table describes available configurations and does not establish that stage attention degrades the final architecture or that block attention alone accounts for its accuracy.')
para('Figure~\\ref{fig:loss_curves} plots',r'Figure~\ref{fig:loss_curves} shows archived loss trajectories over the first 50 epochs. The historical figure retains its original configuration labels, which require reconciliation with the run identities in Table~\ref{tab:components}. It is a qualitative view of optimization traces rather than a matched component experiment.')
para('Across 50 epochs, total and task',r'The displayed losses generally decrease, with transient validation fluctuations. A downward trend does not establish asymptotic convergence, and transient spikes are incompatible with describing every trajectory as monotonic. Because the configurations and task objectives differ, total loss magnitudes cannot be used to rank detector quality across runs.')
para('Temperature-Softmax Bounded Uncertainty Weighting (T-SBUW,',r'T-SBUW constrains the sum and range of task weights and calibrates running loss scales. The trajectories do not isolate its effect from augmentation, architecture, or schedule. A controlled weighting ablation and gradient telemetry would be needed to attribute numerical stability to T-SBUW.')
para('To evaluate training duration',r'The archived T4 sensitivity records compare an AP-selected epoch-130 checkpoint ($71.46\%$ Moderate mAP, $73.42\%$ $\text{mAP}_9$, $60.96\,\text{ms}$ pipeline latency) with a loss-selected epoch-47 checkpoint ($71.48\%$, $73.57\%$, $81.23\,\text{ms}$). These are different checkpoint-selection outcomes from the archived final-run history, rather than independently trained 100- and 50-epoch schedules. Although their aggregate AP is similar, their per-class profiles differ. The $25.0\%$ latency difference is observational; no matched candidate-count or timing experiment assigns it to heatmap sharpening, orientation calibration, or training duration alone.')
para('\\captionof{figure}{Empirical multi-task',r'\captionof{figure}{Archived training and validation loss trajectories over the first 50 epochs. Panels show total training/validation losses and classification, geometry, quality, offset, size, and yaw terms. The historical A-label legend requires reconciliation with the source-linked inventory in Table~\ref{tab:components}. Different objectives and configurations limit direct loss-magnitude comparisons; the curves alone do not establish monotonic convergence or a causal effect of T-SBUW.}')
para('    \\item \\textbf{Focal Heatmap',r'    \item \textbf{Heatmap concentration}: The selected MobilePIXORNeXt panels show more concentrated foreground responses and less visible background activation than the displayed MobilePIXOR panels. This qualitative comparison concerns the complete configurations; it does not isolate Block-SimAM, SG-FPN, or the cause of a post-processing speed difference.')
para('    \\item \\textbf{Distant and Sparse',r'    \item \textbf{Sparse object examples}: Some displayed distant vehicles receive stronger responses from MobilePIXORNeXt. Vehicle examples cannot independently validate the Pedestrian/Cyclist range-AP gains in Table~\ref{tab:distance_ap}, and the selected images do not quantify recall over all validation scenes.')
para('The results showed that accurate 3D',r'The local evaluation supports compact oriented BEV footprint detection without learned point encoders or 3D sparse convolutions. Strata-BEV14 retains point-cloud statistics for a detector with fewer than $0.76\,\text{M}$ deployable parameters. Historical results motivate grouped heads, physical augmentation, and geometric supervision, but their confounded settings do not support an additive attribution of the final accuracy to individual components. The empirical scope is BEV detection; complete 3D cuboid detection and closed-loop safety are not evaluated.')
para('MobilePIXORNeXt is formulated specifically',r'MobilePIXORNeXt predicts horizontal footprints and orientation modulo $\pi$, without vertical extent, directed heading, tracking, or time-to-collision estimation. The empirical study uses KITTI 64-beam LiDAR, leaving sparse sensors, adverse weather, non-planar roads, and embedded platforms untested. Local and reference evaluator outputs require an equivalence study before a common accuracy ranking is justified. Matched component runs, fixed-budget seed repetitions, consistent checkpoint selection, and verified hardware/timing boundaries are needed to resolve the remaining experimental attribution questions. Future work will examine these controls and hardware-specific deployment.')
f.write_text('\n'.join(lines)+'\n')
print('Revised',len(lines),'lines of Results with bounded interpretations.')
